 hundred  dollars more expensive and more  comparable to the touch bar MacBook Pro  we also tested screen brightness and the  MacBook Pro definitely gets a lot  brighter which is really nice for using  in bright rooms or outside and it's also  much more color accurate with p-3 wide  color gamut support as for the design  itself the MacBook shell is machined out  of a solid block of aluminum so it feels  great the XPS on the other hand uses a  lot of plastic sandwiched between an  aluminum case so it's also quite a bit  thicker than the MacBook Pro at its  thickest point but slightly thinner at  the thinnest point and when you stack  them on top of each other it's also  quite a bit smaller for ports the xbs  features three Gen 2 USB C ports with  two of them supporting Thunderbolt 3  alongside a headphone jack in a mini SD  card slot the MacBook is limited to just  two Thunderbolt reports in a headphone  jack so the XPS one's there  they both use USB C for charging but the  XPS a charging cable is built into the  power brick so you can't disconnect it  and use it for data transfer like you  can on the MacBook Pro just like last  year the xB s is a bit tough to open up  since it lacks a notch in the front and  the hinge has a lot of resistance while  the MacBook Pro easily opens out with  just one finger the XPS is power button  doubles as a fingerprint reader so you  can conveniently login without typing in  the password like you have to do on the  MacBook and the keyboard feels pretty  great with a lot of key travel the  MacBook Pro skis are very clicky which I  personally prefer but it's definitely  not as comfortable to type on with the  slow key travel and perhaps the biggest  downside for the MacBook is that it's  using the failure-prone butterfly  keyboard that's been causing Apple a lot  of issues in recent years now for the  track pads the MacBook Pro has the best  trackpad out of any laptop ever and I'm  not exaggerating it doesn't actually  move or click it