ated  at we saw similar results and the more  realistic Cinebench r15 benchmark where  we got almost a hundred more points by  plugging it into power and using best  performance mode but either way the XPS  outperforms a MacBook Pro by a massive  margin and in Geekbench for its graphics  test the XPS is once again scoring  better than the MacBook Pro so we're  really impressed with its performance  but on a positive note the MacBook Pros  SSD storage is quite a bit faster than  the Dells which is useful for everything  from transferring data to opening apps  but the Dell is a clear winner in  overall performance so if you're using  professional apps that can use all that  power you should definitely choose the  Dell but if you just need a laptop to do  basic things like browse the internet  use web-based apps and basic stuff like  that the MacBook Pro will be more than  powerful enough but then again if you're  not using professional apps you really  don't need the MacBook Pro the better  option is the 2018 MacBook Air it's got  newer technology with the t2 chip  fingerprint reader a more comfortable  keyboard better battery life in a better  price so in this case who is the 2017  MacBook Pro for well it's for those who  are using professional apps that are  exclusive to Mac OS but even in this  case  I would seriously recommend just paying  $1,800 for the 2019 MacBook Pro which  just came out last month and has a  quad-core processor this video is  sponsored by PDF Elements 7 which allows  you to create edit convert and much more  on both Windows and Mac PDF Elements 7  has a very simple word like interface so  anyone can easily use it but it's packed  with powerful features like commenting  watermarking batch processing securing  and more it can even transform image  based PDFs into selectable searchable  and editable text it's a great  alternative to Adobe Acrobat at a much  lower price and if you use our link in  the video description you can get 50%  off the no